# Bars that change: Mussorgsky's Promenade from Pictures at an Exhibition, bars of five and six in
# turn, a bar's time signature found, beats counted across changing bars, a bar of five tapped, and
# a limping walk of the child's own.
lesson music-changing-metre v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=6 subject=music levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Bars that change: Mussorgsky's Promenade"
  goal "Count, read and tap music whose bars change length, five beats then six."

  look {
    scene 25x10 {
      countrow row beats=11 meter=0 now=-1 at=canvas(1, 1)
    }
    say """
      Crossing the rope bridge over the gorge, nobody walks in an even step. In 1874 the Russian
      composer Modest Mussorgsky wrote a piano suite, Pictures at an Exhibition, after visiting an
      exhibition of his late friend Viktor Hartmann's pictures. Between the pieces, a Promenade
      walks from picture to picture, and its bars change length: a bar of five beats, then a bar of
      six, again and again. The change of time signature gives the walk its uneven step.
      """
  }
  do {
    show music.metre-which k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show music.metre-beats n=3 easy-n=2 hard-n=6
    level easy {
      worked music.metre-start b=2 level=medium
    }
    show music.metre-start b=3 hard-b=4
    show music.metre-tap
    show music.metre-which k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=3
    show music.metre-beats n=4 easy-n=4 hard-n=7
    show music.metre-years q=0 hard-q=1
    level medium hard {
      show music.metre-start b=4 hard-b=5
    }
    level easy {
      show music.metre-beats n=3
    }
    level hard {
      show music.metre-start b=3 level=medium
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show music.metre-pattern r=0 easy-r=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show music.metre-write v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=2
  }
  remember {
    say "A time signature can change from bar to bar; count each bar by its own number of beats."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that a piece need not keep one time signature: the Promenade alternates bars of five
    and six beats, which gives it an uneven walking step, and counting through it means counting
    each bar by its own number. The step up from grade 6's six-eight, which taught one new metre, is
    metres that change from bar to bar. The wrong ideas to watch for are multiplying every bar by
    the same number, and counting the last beat of a bar as the first of the next. In the working,
    look for the child writing 5, 6, 5, 6 above the bars before adding. The core finds a bar's
    beats, counts beats across changing bars, finds where a bar begins, taps a bar of five and works
    with the dates; the two-star asks how often the pattern comes round, and the three-star is a
    limping walk of the child's own, written and clapped.

    The facts are from Wikipedia, "Pictures at an Exhibition": written for piano in June 1874 after
    a memorial exhibition of over 400 works by Viktor Hartmann, an architect and painter who died in
    1873; ten movements and a recurring Promenade, whose bars alternate 5/4 and 6/4, in B flat
    major; Maurice Ravel's orchestra version of 1922. The bars in the lesson are written to count
    and are not Mussorgsky's. Listen to the opening Promenade and conduct it, five then six. This
    lesson meets China's 2022 arts standard for grade 6, 「对常见节拍以及变化拍子能做出相应的反应」 (respond to common
    metres and to changing metres), as quoted in the audit of grades 5 and 6.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the bars' beats are found from simple notes, the beats are counted over two and four bars, where a bar begins is worked once in the teacher's pen for bar 2 before bar 3, the dates are of the piano suite and Ravel's version, the two-star is as written with its answers in another order, and the rhythm uses crotchets and minims."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the bars' beats include dotted notes and quavers, the beats are counted over six and seven bars, bars 4 and 5 are found, the dates run from Hartmann's death, and the rhythm has a dotted note and four different bars."
  }
}
